\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The centered operator does not commute with restriction}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{On a fine circle of period #{8} and spacing #{a/2}, the character of label #{1} has centered-difference multiplier #{\ui\sin(2\pi /8)/(a/2)=\ui\sqrt{2}/a} by the free centered symbol of \ref{fdip-0142}. Its [restriction](fdip-01BP) to period #{4} has label #{1} and multiplier #{\ui\sin(2\pi /4)/a=\ui /a}. Both characters have value #{1} at zero. The two orders of applying the difference and restricting give different values there. Tensoring with an eigenvector of the Clifford direction gives the same obstruction for the centered spin operator. The Wilson term and any real mass adjustment have real multipliers on that vector, leaving the imaginary discrepancy.}
